% vim: ft=tex
\chapter{CN-VIP}\label{chap:cn-vip}

% chktex-file 25

\margintoc{}

In this chapter, I revisit the \kl{memory action} and \kl{memory operation}
from \fancyref{sec:kernel-mem-action-ops}, and explain the \kl{memory object
model} details I elided initially.

I do so via four key features: dynamic memory allocation and deallocation,
converting to and from byte representations (including support for unions),
copying and comparing arrays of bytes, and lastly allocation liveness and
bounds-checks.

Along the way, I also explain how I proved those rules sound with respect
to an updated model of \kl{ResCore} heaps. I do not prove that these rules are
a sound abstraction above \kl{VIP}, though I gesture towards why I believe
this to be the case (\fancyref{sec:linking}).

\section{\cinline{malloc} and \cinline{free}}

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnAction{}}{%
        \cndruleActionXXAlloc{},
        \cndruleActionXXKillXXDynamic{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for dynamic memory allocation and
        deallocation.}\label{fig:cnvip-mem-action}
\end{figure*}

In \ActionAlloc{}, the \kl{memory action} \sm{[[alloc (pval1, pval2)]]},
for dynamically allocating memory, takes two operands. The first, \sm{[[pval1]]},
is the alignment for the address of the newly created pointer. The second,
\sm{[[pval2]]}, is the size of the allocation. The action itself consumes no
\kl{resources from the context}, and so needs an empty context with an
\sm{[[emp]]} \kl{resource term}. Its \kl{return type} expresses that it returns a
pointer \sm{[[yp]]}, which is constrained by \sm{[[term]]}, whose first part,
\sm{[[aligned ( inj pval1, yp ) ]]}, says the pointer is aligned to \sm{[[pval1]]},
and whose second part, \sm{[[allocv [ yp . alloc_id ] = ( yp . addr , inj pval2 ) ]]},
says the \kl{allocation history} maps the pointer's \kl{allocation ID} to
the base address \sm{[[ yp . addr ]]} and length \sm{[[ pval2 ]]}. The \kl{return
type} also expresses that it returns a logical array of bytes, \sm{[[y]]}. This
array is the output of an \kl{iterated points-to} of bytes starting from the
base pointer \sm{[[yp]]}. The \kl{points-to} is indexed by the new C type
\sm{[[byte]]}, and is uninitialised \sm{[[ Uninit ]]}, to prevent loading from it.
Alongside the \kl{iterated points-to}, the \kl{return type} also mentions
\sm{[[Alloc (yp.id) ( ( yp . addr,  inj pval2 ) )]]}. Though this looks
similar to the constraint (and records the same bounds information), this is a
\kl(allocation){token} which is proof of \emph{liveness} of the allocation, and also
\kl(allocation){permission} to deallocate the newly created allocation.

In \ActionKillDynamic{}, all the \kl(type){resources} are consumed rather than
produced. As such, the \kl{memory action} requires a \kl{resource term} and a
base pointer: \sm{[[ res_term | kill < dyn > ( pval ) ]]}. The length of the
allocation is obtained from the type of the \kl{resource term}, which must be a
pair of an \kl{iterated points-to} of bytes, and an \kl{allocation token}. The
length of the allocation \sm{[[ oarg . len ]]}, obtained from the \kl{allocation
token's} output argument, allows the rule to check the \kl{ownership} of the
entire allocation has been returned without gaps. This is done in premise 4,
where the SMT solver is asked to prove a quantified formula (which, unlike
assuming a quantified formula, is decidable). The base address of the
\kl{allocation token} \sm{[[ oarg . base ]]}, allows the rule to check that the
address of the supplied pointer \sm{[[ inj pval . addr ]]} is in fact, the correct
base address. The \kl{allocation ID} \sm{[[ term ]]}, allows the rule to check
that the \kl{allocation ID} of the base pointer of the \kl{iterated points-to}
\sm{[[ ptrt . alloc_id ]]} and of \sm{[[ pval ]]} all agree. The action just returns
a unit value.

\section{Converting to and from bytes}\label{sec:convert-bytes}

Note that the rules for loads and stores are unchanged. In particular,
\kl{permission to read or write} a location is enough to deduce its enclosing
allocation is live.\sidenote{\kl{Ownership} of out-of-bounds resources is
equivalent to \sm{\mathsf{false}}.}

So how does one load or store arbitrary C types from dynamically
allocated memory? Via new \kl{predicate operations} to convert between
\kl{ownership} at an arbitrary C type, and \kl{ownership} of its representation
as an array of bytes (\cref{fig:cnvip-to-from-bytes}). Converting \emph{to} the byte
representation consumes a \kl{points-to} at any C type, and produces an \kl{iterated
points-to} at an array of bytes. Note that the initialisation status is
preserved: if the consumed C type is a (fully initialised) struct, then the
produced array of bytes is also fully initialised, including at padding bytes,
but the value of the padding bytes will be constrained to be
\coreinline{Unspecified}. Converting \emph{from} the byte representation
requires that all non-padding bytes are not-\coreinline{Unspecified}; this
prevents loading from uninitialised memory and is checked by premise 3, but is
otherwise as one would expect.

I have implemented (but not formalised) memory operations to support casting
between bytes and \cinline{unsigned
char}.\sidenote{\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cn/commit/95c9a40b9e4184ad37519fe116678b877cbc6720}{CN\#95c9a40b}.}
The cast from bytes forbids casting the \coreinline{Unspecified} value; the
cast to bytes is guaranteed to fit within 8 bits by an updated \kl{Cerberus
elaboration}, by wrapping the value appropriately.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnPredOps{}}{%
        \cndrulePredOpsXXToXXBytes{},
        \cndrulePredOpsXXFromXXBytes{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for converting \kl{iterated ownership}
        of bytes to and from \kl{ownership} of other C types.}\label{fig:cnvip-to-from-bytes}
\end{figure*}

\section{Unions and padding}\label{sec:unions-padding}

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cnusedrule{\cndrulePredOpsXXSelect{}}\\
    \cnusedrule{\cndrulePredOpsXXForget{}}\\
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} typing rules for selecting a member
        from a union C type, and for forgetting that the union
        was used at that type.}\label{fig:cnvip-unions}
\end{figure*}

If one has ownership of a pointer to a union, what should the type of the
pointee be, in the specification language? A type-theorist might
suggest an algebraic datatype but the expressiveness of C unions means this
confuses more than it clarifies. C unions support writing to one member and
reading from another member at a different C type and size. How would using
algebraic datatypes support this idiom? How is information written to padding
bytes of a member preserved when switching between the use of union at two
different fields? When a union member is used consistently, would the
programmer need to repeatedly prove the absence of other cases, and unwrap data
for each load?

Another option we considered is to require that ownership of union must state
which member it is being used at. This approach side steps the aforementioned
confusion introduced by algebraic datatypes in specifications, but comes with a
steep price: a loss of a straightforward, compositional reading of ownership of
a struct containing a union. This is a non-starter since such an idiom is
frequently used to encode tagged unions.

To my pleasant surprise, approaching the problem from a \kl{memory object
model} lens, by noticing that what is required of unions is similar to what is
required of bytes, yields an elegant and expressive solution, upon which the
user can encode complex usage patterns for unions.

The solution is tripartite. First, represent \kl{ownership} of a union as
\kl{ownership} of an array of bytes. Second, require a transformation of the
\kl{resource context} to permit reads and writes to a particular member. Third,
record that the member came from a union in a \kl(padding){new token} in
the resource context. In this manner, an array of bytes acts as a least upper
bound of any possible set of members which preserves all the necessary
information, including padding bytes and \kl{provenance} information for
pointers.

\cref{fig:cnvip-unions}, shows how to transform a union \kl{points-to} to and
from \kl{points-to} for a member. Starting with a \kl{points-to} to a union, we
can \sm{\cnkw{select}} a particular member. The C type of the member is
obtained by looking up the definition of the union from its tag in premise
2.\sidenote{See~\cref{sec:metavars-grammar} for further details.} It then
checks that the bytes are valid for that member (that is, not
\coreinline{Unspecified} if a \kl{read-write permission} is desired). If so, the
union \kl{points-to} is transformed into a \kl{points-to} for the member, and
crucially, a \intro[padding token]{token} representing that the \kl{points-to}
came from a union. It can also equivalently be thought of as a predicate
representing ownership of the (potentially empty) padding bytes around the
given member of a union, hence the name \sm{[[ Padding < union tag, member > ]]}.
The inverse transformation checks that the pointers of the \kl{points-to} and
\kl{padding resources} belong to the same allocation at the correct offsets,
and then updates the byte array with the byte representation of the pointee.

\section{\cinline{memcpy} and \cinline{memcmp}}

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnMemop{}}{%
        \cndruleMemopXXMemcpy{},
        \cndruleMemopXXMemcmp{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} \cinline{memcpy} and
        \cinline{memcmp}.}\label{fig:cnvip-memcpy-memcmp}
\end{figure*}

The typing rules for \cinline{memcpy} require \kl{iterated ownership} of two
contiguous arrays of \kl{memory bytes} of length \sm{n}; it returns
\kl(iterated){ownership} of both, with the constraint that the value of the
destination (first) is equal to that of the source (second). The iterations
must be contiguous and of the same length to express the equality constraint on
the values correctly.

The typing rules for \cinline{memcmp} require \kl{iterated ownership} of two
contiguous arrays of \kl{memory bytes} of length \sm{n}; like \cinline{memcpy}, it
returns \kl(iterated){ownership} of both, but unlike
\cinline{memcpy}, none of the bytes being compared must be
\coreinline{Unspecified}, and the returned value is not straightforward to
specify, because the concise or obvious specification would use quantifiers.
Instead, I refer to a recursively defined logical function which constrains the
result to be 0 if all bytes (excluding \kl{provenances}) are equal and the
difference between the first two unequal bytes (excluding \kl{provenances})
otherwise.

\section{Checking pointer shifts}

There are discrepancies between the PNVI-* rules for pointer shifts presented in
\sidetextcite{lepigre2022vip} and \sidetextcite{memarian2022cerberus}, and the
\kl{Cerberus} source code, which I have detailed in
\cref{fig:offset-confusion}.

For \citeauthor{lepigre2022vip}, the omission of any pure/permissive rules is
likely because they are not \kl{memory operations} per se, since they would be
called from the \kl{pure fragment of Core}, even though they are defined by the
\kl{memory object model} (because they are dependent on the representation of
pointers). I suspect the lack of a liveness check for \kl{ISO} array shifting
is likely an error, since the other sources require it.

For \citeauthor{memarian2022cerberus}, I suspect having a special case on
\cinline{NULL} but not for 0-offset is likely an error, given the behaviour
of the code.

For the \kl{Cerberus} code, the pure/permissive member shift has a comment
saying that member shifting a \cinline{NULL} pointer may be \kl{UB} per the
\kl{standard}, but is the assumed behaviour of some GCC torture tests. The result is
\cinline{NULL} if the offset \sm{n} is zero, and is a pointer \sm{(@\mathsf{empty},
n)} if the offset \sm{n} is non-zero. This behaviour is not controlled by any
switch. My conjecture is that member shifting on \cinline{NULL} is merely
allowed to define \cinline{offsetof} as a macro.

The effectful version of member shifting, is actually only used by its CHERI
switch, and is not relevant to capturing the ISO rule.

Instead, \kl{Cerberus} enforces the checks required by the \kl{ISO} rule via
the elaboration. If strict pointer arithmetic is enabled, the member shift
guarded by the \kl{memory operation} \sm{\cnkw{PtrValidForDeref}}. This
ensures the initial pointer is strictly in bounds of a live allocation for a
given C type; any subsequent shifts at that C type are thus guaranteed to
remain in bounds.

Similarly, the effectful, \kl{ISO} version of array shifting is controlled by
the same switch; the elaboration only uses the \kl{memory operation} if strict
pointer arithmetic is enabled, and uses the \kl{pure Core expression}
otherwise.

I chose the following behaviour for the \kl{CN-VIP} formalisation;
I discuss the implementation in~\cref{sec:impl-array-member-shift}.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Pure/permissive member shifts require the pointer to have a
        \kl{provenance}, but do not special-case for the \cinline{NULL}
        pointer. If my conjecture is correct, then any cases which require this
        may be replaced directly with \cinline{offsetof} as a built-in
        construct (one which is already supported by \kl{Cerberus} and
        \kl{CN}).
    \item \kl{ISO} member shifts are not handled directly, because they
        do not show up as such in the elaboration. Instead, the rules
        for \MemopPtrValidForDeref{} and \MemopPtrUnsureForDeref{} allow the
        \kl{ResCore} program to reflect the presence or absence of a live and
        sufficiently large allocation in the resource context into the returned
        value.
    \item Pure/permissive array shifts require the pointer to have a
        provenance.
    \item \kl{ISO} array shifts require the resulting pointers to be in bounds
        of a live allocation.
\end{itemize}

\begin{table*}[tpb]
  \begin{tabular}{ccccc}
  \toprule
   & \citeauthor{lepigre2022vip} incl.\ appendix & \citeauthor{memarian2022cerberus} & Cerberus code & CN-VIP \\% & VIP code \\
  \midrule
  Member (P)
    & {\ballotX✗}
    & case \cinline{NULL}
    & case \cinline{NULL}, 0-offset
    & has provenance
    % & has provenance
  \\
  Member (ISO)
    & bounds, case 0-offset
    & bounds, liveness
    & case \cinline{NULL}, 0-offset
    & {\ballotX✗}
    % & has provenance
  \\
