\end{table*}

\section{Pointer liveness and bounds checks}

Proof that a pointer is live takes one of two forms: either an \kl{allocation
token}, or a \kl{points-to}, whose \kl{allocation ID} matches that of the
pointer. Once that is established, a pointer needs to be checked to ensure it
is within bounds; unlike liveness, this requires only the SMT solver. Both of
these ideas, and their use in \cinline[breaklines]{copy_alloc_id}, are
formalised in~\cref{fig:cnvip-liveness-bounds}. It checks that the result is in
the bounds of the live allocation indicated by its argument. The judgements are
also used in~\cref{fig:mem-model-dyn-ptr-relop}, where the rule for relational
operators on pointers (excluding in/equality, handled separately) checks
whether each argument is in bounds of the same live allocation.

\begin{figure*}[tp]
    \small
    \raggedright{}
    \cndefnLive{}
    \cndefnBounds{}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnMemop{}}{%
        \cndruleMemopXXCopyAllocId{}
    }
    \caption{\kl{Kernel CN} liveness and bounds
        checks.}\label{fig:cnvip-liveness-bounds}
\end{figure*}

