\section{CN-VIP soundness}\label{sec:cn-vip-soundness}

Only a few steps are needed to update the formalisation presented in
\fancyref{chap:kernel-soundness} to use \kl{CN-VIP}.

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Extend the configuration of the dynamic semantics to be a step
        relation between abstract \emph{\kl{states}} and expressions, rather than
        just \emph{\kl{heaps}} and expressions.
    \item Extend the heap typing rules to incorporate the newly added
        allocation history, \sm{[[ Alloc ]]} and
        \sm{[[Padding < ct , member >]]} tokens.
    \item Update the proof of soundness for \kl{resource term} reduction and
        pattern-matching, with the new rules.
\end{enumerate}

I do not interpret and prove sound \kl{ResCore} abstract \kl{state} and
transitions above \kl{VIP} (with non-deterministic pointer equality, and more
permissive pointer to integer casts), though I will explain informally why I
believe this to be the case. Instead, I implement and empirically validate the
design by adapting and running the \kl{VIP} test suite
(\fancyref{sec:insufficiency}).

\subsection{Extending the dynamic semantics}

I modelled the \kl{allocation history} as a single global \kl{logical variable}
\sm{[[allocv]]}. This means that even morally closed programs have that variable
free in constraints and \kl{resource terms}. At the same time, because the
\kl{allocation history} is extended during the course of evaluating a
\kl{ResCore} program, it is not a term which can be substituted once at the
start of the program. Hence, it is threaded throughout the dynamic semantics,
and substituted in at the point of calling the SMT solver
(\cref{fig:mem-model-dyn-smt}).

\begin{marginfigure}
    \small%
    \cndefnSubsXXSMT{}
    \caption{Calls to the SMT solver are now extended to thread through the
        changing \kl{allocation history}.}\label{fig:mem-model-dyn-smt}
\end{marginfigure}

Only the \sm{\cnkw{create}} and \sm{\cnkw{alloc}} extend the
\kl{allocation history}, and so it and every \kl(effectful){expression}
containing it also include the \kl{allocation history} as part of its
configuration, rather than threaded through the side. Note that because I split
the \kl{intuitionistic} part of the \kl{allocation history} from the
\kl{linear} part, it does not get updated to record a dead allocation in the
rule for \sm{\cnkw{kill}} (\cref{fig:mem-model-dyn-create-kill}).

\begin{figure*}
    \small%
    \raggedright%
    \begingroup%
    \NewCommandCopy{\origcndruleOpXXActionXXCreate}{\cndruleOpXXActionXXCreate}
    \renewcommand{\cndruleOpXXActionXXCreate}{\onlyUseNthCnPremise{1,2,3,4,5,9,10}{\origcndruleOpXXActionXXCreate{}}}
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnOpXXAction{}}{%
        \cndruleOpXXActionXXCreate{},
        \cndruleOpXXActionXXKillXXStatic{}
    }
    \endgroup%
    \caption{The \kl{allocation history} only tracks a mapping from IDs to a
        pair of base address and size, so when an allocation is killed,
        existing entries are not mutated.}\label{fig:mem-model-dyn-create-kill}
\end{figure*}

With the exception of threading through the \kl{allocation history}, the rules
for loads and stores are unchanged. The rules for converting \kl{ownership} of
objects into \kl{iterated ownership} of memory bytes and vice versa are
\kl{predicate operations}, much like the ones for manipulating structs and
fixed-length arrays. The rules for \sm{\cnkw{memcpy}} and \sm{\cnkw{memcmp}}
are also as expected.

\kl{Pointer operations}, including casts and \cinline[breaklines]{copy_alloc_id}, do
not extend the \kl{allocation history}, but do require the \kl{heap} to check
whether the supplied or resulting pointers belong to live allocations. They are
agnostic of whether it is \kl{ownership} or an \sm{\cnkw{Alloc}} token is
provided as evidence (\cref{fig:mem-model-dyn-ptr-relop}).

\begin{figure*}
    \small%
    \raggedright%
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnMemop{}}{%
        \cndruleMemopXXRelXXBinop{}
    }
    \onlyUseRules{\cndefnOpXXMemop{}}{%
        \cndruleOpXXMemopXXRelXXBinop{}
    }
    \caption{Memory operations involving pointers perform a bounds check using
        the SMT solver and supplied pointers, and a liveness check based on
        evidence from the supplied resource term and the heap.}\label{fig:mem-model-dyn-ptr-relop}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{State typing}

\kl{State typing} is a generalisation of \kl{heap typing}
(\S\ref{sec:heap-types}) to include the append-only \kl{allocation history}.

First, the \kl{allocation history} needs to be ``well-constrained'':
\sm{[[alloct <= N]]} (\cref{fig:alloc-typing}) means that each constraint
in \sm{[[N]]}, with the \kl{allocation history} \sm{[[alloct]]} substituted for
\sm{[[allocv]]}, holds.

\begin{marginfigure}
    \footnotesize%
    \raggedright%
    \cndefnAlloc{}%
    \caption{Definition of a well-constrained \kl{allocation history}
        \textemdash{} $[[alloct]]$ is consistent with each constraint in
        \kl(constraint){context} $[[N]]$.}\label{fig:alloc-typing}
\end{marginfigure}

\kl{Heap typing} generalises similarly (\cref{fig:heap2-typing}). It
substitutes the \kl{allocation history} \sm{[[alloct]]} for \sm{[[allocv]]}, and then
checks the \kl{heap} exactly as before.

\begin{marginfigure}
    \footnotesize%
    \raggedright%
    \cndefnHeapPP{}%
    \caption{Definition of \kl(state){heap typing} in the presence of an
        \kl{allocation history}: substitute the latter into the \kl{heap} and
        the \kl{normalised resource context}, and proceed as
        before.}\label{fig:heap2-typing}
\end{marginfigure}

\subsection{Updating the soundness proof}

Recall that I defined \kl{resource term reduction} and
\kl(resource){pattern-matching} in a big-step style (\S\ref{sec:heap-types}).
Whilst this intertwines the proofs for progress and type preservation, its
advantage of modularity pays off now.

The new constructs such as non-deterministic pointer equality, \sm{\cnkw{alloc}},
dynamic \sm{\cnkw{kill}}, \sm{\cnkw{memcpy}},
\sm{\cnkw{memcmp}}, \cinline{copy_alloc_id}, and the conversions to and
from memory bytes, are just additional cases in the proof, the rest are merely
updates.

The updates are small because the additional constraints on the \kl{allocation
history} are easy to link across the static and dynamic semantics by the
definition of \kl{allocation history typing}. The bounds and liveness checks
are similarly easy to link across the static and dynamic semantics by the
definition of \kl(state){heap typing}.

The updated theorem statements are as below: the main differences is the use of
\kl{state typing}, and the \kl{logical context} \sm{[[L0]] = [[allocv : id_map ]]}.
Updated proofs are in~\cref{subsec:res_term_progress}.

\begin{theorem}[CN-VIP:\ progress and type preservation for resource terms]
For all `closed' \kl{resource terms} (\sm{[[ res_term ]]}) which type \kl{check}
or \kl{synthesise} (\sm{[[ cdot ; L0 ; N ; nR |- res_term <= res ]]}), and
\kl{well-typed states} (\sm{[[ alloct ; h <= N ; nR ]]}), there exists a
\kl{resource value} (\sm{[[ res_val ]]}), \kl(normalised){context} (\sm{[[ nR' ]]})
and \kl{heap} (\sm{[[ h' ]]}), such that: the \kl(resource){value} is well-typed
(\sm{[[ cdot ; L0 ; N ; nR' |- res_val <= res ]]}); the \kl{heap is well-typed}
(\sm{[[ alloct |- h' <= nR' ]]}), and for all frame-\kl{heaps} (\sm{[[ f ]]}), the
\kl{resource term} reduces to the \kl{resource value} without affecting the
frame-\kl{heap} (\sm{[[ alloct | < h + f ; res_term > ||v < h' + f ; res_val > ]]}).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Progress for ResCore]
If a \kl[stmt]{statement} (\sm{[[ stmt ]]}) is well-typed
(\sm{[[ cdot ; L0 ; N ; nR |- stmt <= ret ]]}) and all \kl{computational patterns}
in it are exhaustive, then either it is a \kl(return){value} (\sm{[[ < ret_terms > ]]}), or it is
unreachable, or for all \kl{well-typed states} (\sm{[[ s <= N ; nR ]]})
then there exists another \kl{state} (\sm{[[ s' ]]}) and \kl[stmt]{statement} (\sm{[[ stmt' ]]})
which is stepped to (\sm{[[ < s ; stmt > --> < s' ; stmt' > ]]})
in the dynamic semantics.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Type preservation for ResCore]
For all closed and well-typed \kl[stmt]{statements}
(\sm{[[ cdot ; L0 ; N ; nR |- stmt <= ret ]]}),
\kl{well-typed states} (\sm{[[ alloct ; h <= N ; nR ]]}),
frame-\kl{heaps} (\sm{[[ f ]]}),
new states (\sm{[[ alloct' ; h' ]]}),
and new statements (\sm{[[ stmt' ]]}),
which are connected by a step in the dynamic semantics
(\sm{[[ < alloct ; h + f ; stmt > -->  < alloct' ; heap ; stmt' > ]]}),
if all top-level functions are annotated correctly,
there exists a \kl{constraint context} (\sm{[[ N' ]]}),
sub-\kl{heap} (\sm{[[ h' ]]}),
and \kl(normalised){resource context} (\sm{[[ nR' ]]}),
such that the \kl{constraint context} is extended
% chktex-file 15
(\sm{[[ cdot ; L0 ; N ; cdot [= cdot ; L0 ; N' ; cdot ]]}), % chktex 9
the frame is unaffected (\sm{[[ heap ]] = [[ h' + f ]]}),
the sub-\kl{state is well-typed} (\sm{[[ alloct' ; h' <= N' ; nR' ]]}),
and the \kl[stmt]{statement} too
(\sm{[[ cdot ; L0 ; N' ; nR' |- stmt' <= ret ]]}).
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Linking \kl{CN-VIP} to \kl{VIP}}\label{sec:linking}

By introducing a new C type for bytes, \kl{CN-VIP} departs from \kl{VIP}.

Bytes are intended only for transforming C types to and from their
representation, critical for supporting dynamic memory allocation and unions.
For the formalisation, I only support these transformation at the \kl(type){resource}
level, but for the implementation, I went further, and added explicit casting
to and from bytes and integers at the value level, as new \kl{memory
operations} in the \kl{Cerberus elaboration}.

Pointers to C types can be converted to and from pointers to bytes, but as
values, bytes are new, neither pointer nor integer. Thus the soundness of the
existing integer and pointer memory operations are unaffected by their
introduction.

Load and stores do deal with values, converting to and from their
representation as memory bytes. The introduction of bytes in \kl{CN-VIP} may
raise concerns about increasing expressiveness beyond what is possible
\kl{VIP}, because bytes capture more information. In actuality, \kl{CN-VIP}
bytes are \sm{\sim} related to \kl{VIP} \cinline{char} as follows:
\sm{\mathsf{unspec} \sim \mathsf{unspec}}, \sm{(@\mathsf{empty}, a) \sim
\mathit{Int}\,(a)}, and \sm{(@i, a) \sim \mathit{Loc}\,(@i, a)}.

\kl{Exposure tracking} (not formalised or implemented) would complicate the
relationship between \kl{CN-VIP} and \kl{VIP}: neither would be strictly more
expressive than the other. The former is more restrictive for byte-related
features, requiring \emph{more} annotations (explicit casts to and from bytes).
The latter would be more restrictive for round-trip related features, requiring
changes to the source program, by threading pointers through and using them in
\cinline{copy_alloc_id}s.

% Roundtrip values are also new values, neither pointers nor integers, and
% abstract from the perspective of the C programmer. Thus the soundness of
% various integer and pointer memory operations (excluding casts) are unaffected
% by their introduction. The new \kl{CN-VIP} casts involving roundtrip types, are
% simulated by casting to specific constructors in \kl{VIP}. For pointers,
% $\mathsf{NULL} \sim \mathsf{NULL}$, and $(\pi, a) \sim (\pi, a)$. For roundtrip
% types, $(\pi, 0) \sim \mathit{Int}\,(0)$ and $(\pi, a) \sim \mathit{Loc}\,(\pi, a)$
% for $a \neq 0$. For integer types, $0 \sim \mathit{Int}\,(0)$ and $a \sim
% \mathit{Int}\,(a)$ for $a \neq 0$.


\chapter{Epilogue on CN-VIP}\label{chap:epilogue-cn-vip}

\margintoc%
%
In this chapter, I discuss the impact of transitioning the \kl{CN} test
and tutorial suite to using \kl{CN-VIP}. I finish with a discussion on how
future work could use the principles uncovered by \kl{CN-VIP} to make
it and \kl{CN} overall more trustworthy.

\section{Performance}\label{sec:vip-perf}

\kl{CN-VIP} degrades \kl{CN}'s performance significantly. The benchmarks on the
\kl{CN} test suite (\cref{fig:vip-performance-hit2}) show a 2\textendash{}3x
slowdown, though this is imperfect because some tests are intended to fail in
early stages such as parsing, desugaring or well-formedness checks.

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1.5\textwidth]{figures/cnvip-performance.png}
    \caption{Benchmarks for \kl{CN}'s test suite, with time in seconds on the
        y-axis and commits on the x-axis. It shows a 2\textendash{}3x increase
        in execution time when enabling \kl{CN-VIP}, followed by a 50\%
        decrease for Z3 after an optimisation, courtesy of
        \url{https://rems-project.github.io/cerberus/dev/bench/}.}\label{fig:vip-performance-hit2}
\end{figure*}

% It is more fruitful to look at the tests individually, and see which ones
% show a demonstrable slowdown when \kl{VIP} was enabled.
%
% \begin{itemize}
%     \item block\_type.c (CVC5, 4x)
%     \item implies2.error.c (CVC5, 2x)
%     \item extract\_verbose.c (CVC5, 5x)
%     \item builtin\_ctz.c (CVC5, <2x)
%     \item disj\_nonnull.c (both, <2x)
%     \item gnu\_types\_compatible.c (CVC, <2x)
%     \item tree16/as\_partial\_map/tree16.c (both, 3/4x)
%     \item tree16/as\_mutual\_map/tree16.c (both, 3x)
%     \item fun\_ptr\_extern.c (CVC5, 4x)
%     \item increments.c (both, 10x)
%     \item has\_alloc\_id\_shift.c (both, <2x)
%     \item int\_to\_ptr.c (both, <2x)
%     \item has\_alloc\_id\_ptr\_neq.c (both, <2x)
%     \item fun\_ptr\_three\_opts.c (Z3 <2x, CVC5 4x)
%     \item has\_alloc\_id\_ptr\_eq.error.c (CVC5 2x)
%     \item ptr\_diff2.error.c (both <2x)
%     \item ptr\_relop.error.c (both 2/3x)
%     \item ptr\_diff.errro.c (Z3 <2x, CVC5 3x)
%     \item bitwise\_compl.c (CVC5 2x)
%     \item alloc\_token.c (both <2x)
%     \item implies\_precedence.c (CVC5 2x)
%     \item division\_casting.c (Z3 <2x, CVC5 2x)
%     \item reverse.c (Z3 6x, CVC5 5x)
%     \item mask\_ptr.c (Z3 <2x, CVC5 3x)
%     \item and\_or\_precedence.error.c (CVC5 2x)
%     \item get\_from\_arr.c (Z3 2x, CVC5 3x)
%     \item enum\_and\_and.c (both 2x)
%     \item simplify\_array\_shift.c (CVC5 5x, Z3 7x)
% \end{itemize}

% There are three potential reasons for this.
% \begin{enumerate}
%     \item Pointer bounds checks.
%     \item Pointer liveness checks.
%     \item Deriving constraints.
% \end{enumerate}

The implementation of \kl{CN-VIP} has two primary components: the pointer
liveness checks, and the bounds checks. Disabling the liveness checks is simple
and gives sensible timing information: tests which passed before will continue
to do so; tests which failed because pointers could not be proved live are
small ones which do not test anything afterwards. Disabling liveness has barely
any impact on the total time taken (a slight increase, because of previously
failing tests now passing) and so what remains is the bounds checks.

The bounds checks are easy to disable, but this does not lead to a fair
comparison: of course we expect the version which checks less and unsoundly to
go faster. Furthermore, any slowdown for bounds checks will be some mixture of
the effect of derived constraints, and the complexity of the check itself.

The above two facts combined suggest a possible remedy: if any bounds checks
can be replaced with checks in the \kl{resource context}, then it may offer a
substantial speed-up. Since \kl{ownership} of a pointer is sufficient (but not
necessary) to deduce bounds checks, we can replace some of them with checks in
the \kl{resource context} for \kl{ownership}.

In particular, for member and array shifts, this is a good strategy, because
often the shifting is done just before loading, for which ownership needs to be
present regardless. The ownership check can be done first, and if it fails, the
code will fall back to the slower, full check.

The net result of this is a substantial mitigation \kl{CN-VIP} of the initial
degradation, though only for Z3 and not CVC5: a 3x slowdown is now less than 2x
slowdown. \kl{CN-VIP} gives an optimisation, but takes a long time regardless.

This begs the question: why is a linear lookup in the \kl{resource context}
faster than proving a pure SMT constraint? One possibility is that most
equalities tested in \kl(type){resource} lookup can be resolved syntactically.
Another possibility is that the encoding of pointer arithmetic (producing an
unconstrained value for shifting on \coreinline{NULL}) and bounds-checks (based
on pairwise disjointness for each points-to in the context) is sub-optimal. In
practice, we have observed that, \emph{not} automatically deriving disjointness
constraints can speed-up verification
significantly.\sidenote{\url{https://github.com/rems-project/cn-pKVM-hyp-allocator/pull/13}}

It is possible to optimise further, for example, the \kl{Core} elaboration
performs a \coreinline{PtrValidForDeref} check before every
\coreinline{member_shift}, and so the checks on the \coreinline{member_shift}
itself are technically redundant. Though perhaps a bit fragile, such facts
could be exploited (with a switch) to improve performance of
\kl{CN-VIP} even more.

\section{Updating existing code}

I developed \kl{CN-VIP} piecemeal, gated by a flag. \kl{CN-VIP} is stricter
than the previous concrete model, and so it was easy to update tests in a
backwards-compatible manner. For new features, I added new tests (a mix of
positive and negative ones), separate from the existing regression suite.

To quantify the impact of \kl{CN-VIP} on existing code, I used the (over 200)
\kl{CN tutorial} examples. As the
\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cn-tutorial/commit/9ee153e74d1b0fbdcb2802f2186d211ab6b2343b}{`Add
support for VIP} commit demonstrates, out of roughly 185 small,
``working'' test cases, only 7 had to be updated.\sidenote{A limitation of the
    testing framework for the tutorial, and at the time, the \kl{CN} regression
    suite, is that it only checks return codes, not the whole output. So there
    was no checking for the changes in the reason for a failing test, which I
    later fixed
    (\href{https://github.com/rems-project/cerberus/pull/703}{Cerberus\#703})
    by capturing and automatically diffing all output.} Four of them were
related to casting integers to pointers, of which one is a round-trip. All of
them were updated using \cinline{copy_alloc_id}.

% \inputminted[fontsize=\footnotesize,breaklines,firstline=10,lastline=68]{diff}{code/add_support_for_vip.patch}

%\subsection{Catching \kl{UB}, as intended}

The other three were all related to \emph{decrementing} a pointer out of
bounds, and technically \kl{UB}. Assuming the intent was to test pointer
decrementing rather than catching \kl{UB}, I amended the code to to sidestep
\kl{UB}.

% \inputminted[fontsize=\footnotesize,breaklines,firstline=69,lastline=107]{diff}{code/add_support_for_vip.patch}

\subsection{Non-deterministic pointer equality}\label{subsec:update-non-det-ptr-eq}

The other set of examples were the approximately 81 C files (114 including
headers) used in the \kl{CN tutorial} itself. Of these, 7 were intentionally broken in
non-\kl{CN-VIP} ways for pedagogical reasons, which I manually checked
remained so after the switch. Another 4 were intentionally without
annotations, of which 3 started to fail verification earlier because of
\kl{CN-VIP} related reasons (member shifting on a pointer not guaranteed to be
non-\cinline{NULL}). Of the remaining 70 C files (103 including headers), only
2 predicate definitions (in headers) and 2 \kl{lemmas} needed updating. The latter
followed from the former because the \kl{lemmas} inlined the definitions of
the \kl(user-defined){predicates}.

% \inputminted[fontsize=\footnotesize,breaklines,firstline=108]{diff}{code/add_support_for_vip.patch}

All those updates were related to excluding the \kl{non-deterministic
case in pointer equality} in the queue example (\S\ref{chap:example-queue}).

Specifically, because the implementation for the pop operation, and the C proof
of an induction \kl{lemma} to verify the push operation, both used C's
\kl(non-det){pointer equality}, the \kl(user-defined){predicates} for it needed
to constrain the relevant pointers to ensure address equality implied pointer
equality. This is enough to assuage \kl{CN-VIP} that the
\kl(ptr-eq){non-deterministic} case is impossible. The constraint is sensible
(and the example is still usable) because the pointers involved refer to a node
in a queue; thus, they need to be valid for dereferencing (not \kl{one-past}),
hence ruling out the possibility that they could have equal addresses and
differing \kl{provenances}.

\section{\kl{Lemma} proofs within C}\label{sec:lemma-proof-c}

The \kl{CN tutorial} queue example requires the use of \kl{lemmas} to prove the
appropriate facts for popping and pushing. I did not write the implementation
for these examples, but I did verify them, including proofs of \kl{lemmas} using C
as a tactic language, with the caveat they do not have or check a termination
metric on recursive C functions.

The experience was humbling because I found it quite difficult; it took a few
days work. This was mostly due to my lack of prior experience with \kl{separation
logic} proofs about basic data-structures such as linked lists, and resulting
lack of intuition, however some of this did come down to deciphering often
inscrutable \kl{counter-examples}.

% If the target audience of \kl{CN} is competent programmers with little to no
% experience in verification, then the bar for usability and pedagogy needs to be
% accordingly high. A verification expert will find the tool rough around the
% edges but useful, but a novice will find it frustrating and miss the value of
% verification.

Let \cinline{snoc} be the \kl(CN){function} which adds an element to the back
of a list. It is recursive, and so will require \kl[CN statement]{hints}
from the user to prove facts about it. The \kl{lemma} for popping (below)
states that the head and the tail of a \cinline{snoc}'d list is the head of the
original list, and the \cinline{snoc} of the original tail. A limitation of the
syntax \textemdash{} the inability to have an assertion about a
\kl(type){resource} in the middle of a C function and bind its output
\textemdash{} means that we are forced to mention \kl(type){resources} to get a
handle on the \kl{logical values} of their \kl(arg){output}.

\cfile[fontsize=\footnotesize,breaklines,highlightlines={2-12}]{code/queue_pop_lemma.h} % chktex 8

The \kl{lemma} for pushing however (seen in the diff above), is more
interesting, because it requires induction on a \kl{user-defined predicate},
rather than a \kl{logical} (\kl{ghost}) data-structure. It says that if you
have ownership of a list segment from pointers \cinline{front} to \cinline{p},
and \kl{ownership} of node \cinline{struct queue_cell} at \cinline{p}, then you
can reinterpret that as ownership of a larger queue from \cinline{front} to
\cinline{p->next}, and interpret the \kl{logical} queue it represents as a
\cinline{snoc} of the original queue and \cinline{p->first}.

The \kl{lemma} proved here includes an extra parameter and constraint on line
10 as a sanity check at the call site. It also includes \kl{ownership} of the
last node on line 11, which turns out to be necessary to prove the constraint
on line 14.

\cfile[fontsize=\footnotesize,breaklines,firstline=3,lastline=29,highlightlines={7-18,22,26}]{code/queue_push_induction.c} % chktex 8

The proof itself is a recursive C function, which follows the structure
of the \cinline{QueueAux} predicate (\cref{fig:queue-aux-def}). In particular,
it can be seen as traversing the queue until the base case and adding the extra
\cinline[breaklines]{struct queue_cell}.

\begin{marginfigure}
    \cfile[fontsize=\footnotesize,breaklines,highlightlines={1-15}]{code/queue_aux.h} % chktex 8
    \caption{\cninline{QueueAux} \kl(user-defined){predicate} which states
        ownership of a linked list of \cinline{queue_cell}s from
        \cninline{front} inclusive to \cninline{back} exclusive. The
        \kl{ownership} for \cinline{back} is not included because that needs to
        be claimed earlier for constant time updates.}\label{fig:queue-aux-def}
\end{marginfigure}

To be clear: \emph{this C \kl{lemma} or proof is not intended to be executed at
runtime}. Indeed that would defeat the point of a queue with a pointer to the
back for constant time pushing. Instead, it demonstrates that there is a class
of resource \kl{lemmas} that are provable without appeal to the underlying
{memory object model}.

\section{\kl{Lemma} proofs in a proof-assistant}\label{sec:lemma-prover}

In our use so far, the only use case that falls outside the aforementioned
class of \kl{lemmas} it to work around the limitations of using the
\kl{decidable fragment of SMT theories}, \eg{} the mismatch between the
finite arrays represented by \kl{iterated predicates}, and the total maps used
in \kl{decidable SMT theories} (\cref{fig:byte-array-eq-lemma}).

% Using that, it would be possible to build support on merging array values.
% Non-SMT related examples have not come up quite yet, but it is difficult to
% rule them out and say they will never come up.

\kl{CN} already has a mechanism, implemented by Thomas Sewell, for exporting
\kl{lemmas} about pure facts to Rocq; this was used extensively in an earlier
version \textemdash{} with integers instead of bit-vectors \textemdash{} to
reason about the bit manipulations done by the \kl{buddy allocator} in
\kl{pKVM} (\S\ref{chap:buddy}). It supports exporting pure facts involving
datatypes too.

This feature was extended by Amy Yin to support exporting and proving \kl{lemmas}
involving resources. Because of \kl{CN}'s aim (\S\ref{sec:cn-goals}) to behave
like a decidable type system rather than a program logic, there will always be
limits to the expressiveness of the type system, and the degree of automation
which is pragmatic.

At the same time, we would like the logic used for proving \kl{lemmas} to be
consistent with the (intent of the) OCaml implementation, to prevent users from
bypassing verification errors with a lemma. An obvious candidate for this is
the Iris~\sidecite{jung2018iris} framework in Rocq, which allows users to
define their own notion of resources and inherit many powerful proof rules
based on that.

However, Iris requires an operational semantics over which to prove the basic
primitives sound, on top of which other rules are defined. Note that the
current resource \kl{lemma} export and proof mechanism sidesteps this issue and
focuses on translating \kl(logical){function} and \kl{predicate definitions}
and \kl{lemma} statements.

Another impediment is that Iris is an \kl{affine} separation logic, whereas we
would like to work in a \kl{linear} setting, for which the work
of~\sidetextcite{bizjak2019iron} might be useful.

However, putting \kl{Core} and its dynamic semantics inside Rocq, would be a
significant duplication of work (it is already implemented in \kl{Cerberus})
and difficult (in particular the highly non-compositional rules for
\sm{\cnkw{run}}/\sm{\cnkw{save}} and weak sequencing). The effort and risk is large
and clear, but the reward is not so. Supporting \kl(type){resourceful}
\kl{lemmas} seems to pull on a thread which drags with it all of \kl{Cerberus}.
Is there an alternative?

We considered using a ``faithful subset'' of \kl{Core} to make the project
feasible, but how this would work, or integrate into the current project, was
very unclear. We also considered just continuing with pen-and-paper proofs of
soundness for richer logic, but the would just duplicate Iris, in a more
error-prone manner.

This impasse can be broken with a few observations:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Currently, \kl(type){resources} and \kl(resource){contexts} are
        defined and interpreted only with respect to a chosen \kl{memory object
        model}.
    \item A \kl{lemma} is a purely ghost state operation (a ``view shift'' in Iris
        terminology), and so would entail proving that the
        \emph{interpretation} of the sub-\kl{heap} it consumes is the same as
        the \emph{interpretation} of the sub-\kl{heap} it produces.
\end{itemize}

With this, a \kl{lemma} is sound if the interpretations of its \kl{pre-} and
\kl{postconditions} are equal. The interpretations are defined to be exactly as
in \fancyref{sec:linking}.

\section{Better foundations for CN}\label{sec:better-foundations}

If \kl{CN} \emph{users} should be prevented from introducing unsoundness via
\kl{lemmas}, should \kl{CN} \emph{implementers} be treated similarly
for the implementation itself?

Putting all of \kl{Core} and its dynamic semantics inside Rocq would be
sufficient, but is it necessary? I conjecture that neither symbolically
evaluating \kl{Core}, nor using SMT solvers, are the most error-prone parts of
the system: \kl{Core} is a relatively simple first order language, and the SMT
solvers are better tested than \kl{CN}'s resource context manipulations.

Those manipulations are well-defined, at least in principle, if not in the
implementation: they are exactly the ones described by the grammar of
\kl{ResCore} \kl{resource terms} (\S\ref{sec:res-terms}), plus adding and
\kl(type){resources} to the \kl(resource){context}. This is promising, but
\kl{let-normalising} \kl{Core} and adding \kl{explicit resource terms} is still
impractical.

% \begin{itemize}
%     \item At the start of a C function, add \kl(type){resources} from the
%         \kl{precondition} to the \kl(resource){context}.
%     \item At the end of a C function, check that the \kl(resource){context}
%         and the \kl{postcondition} mention the same \kl(type){resources}.
%     \item Before a C function call, find and remove from the
%         \kl(resource){context} the \kl(type){resources} required by the
%         \kl{precondition} of the callee.
%     \item After a C function call, adding resources from the postcondition to the
%         context.
%     \item After a \coreinline{create*}, adding resources to the context.
%     \item After a \coreinline{kill*}, removing resources from the context.
%     \item Before a \coreinline{kill*}, \coreinline{load}, \coreinline{store}
%         and many pointer operations, looking up resources in the context.
%     \item After a \coreinline{store}, updating the resource context.
%     \item During any lookup, changing the syntactic structure of resources via
%         folding and unfolding predicates, decomposing and rebuilding pairs,
%         structs, fixed-length arrays and byte representations, and indexing and
%         splitting iterated predicates.
% \end{itemize}
%
% This should be very familiar because \emph{these events are precisely the
% places where \kl{ResCore} requires resource term annotations: memory actions,
% memory operations, pattern matching, function calls and return values.}
% Furthermore, the possible changes on the syntactic structure of resources that
% could occur during a lookup are \emph{precisely the ones described by the
% grammar of resource terms} (\S\ref{sec:res-terms}), indeed that was what they
% were designed for.
%

A more realistic and gradual approach is to instrument the \kl{CN}
implementation, but to record a tree of traces of the \kl{resource context}.
Each event would correspond to a transition between pairs of \kl{CN-VIP}
abstract \kl{states}, corresponding to one \kl{resource term}, upon which an
Iris instantiation could be defined and proven sound. The instantiation could
then be used to construct proofs that each path through the tree of events is
sound. The traces would need to include logical facts proved using the solver,
since those would turn into assumptions or proof obligations in Rocq. Indeed,
the feasibility of this approach has already been partly demonstrated
by~\sidetextcite{zaliva2025bridging}, which (as a first step) records and
verifies all resource lookups and predicate unfolds in Rocq.

Once traces are verified, the boundary of trust would shift away from the
pen-and-paper proofs discussed in this thesis, and towards trust in how well
the OCaml implementation implements the theory. The next step would be to
refactor the implementation so that it becomes impossible to manipulate the
\kl{resource context} without it being recorded in a trace. Thus the
instrumentation would not need to be updated every time the inference algorithm
changes.

Once all the manipulations are thus encapsulated, we could replace the
implementation with one defined in and extracted from Rocq. It would be an
implementation of a \kl{resource context} data-structure, with each operation
on it corresponding to \kl{resource term}. The soundness of each operation
would be proved correct with respect to a mechanised definition of \kl{CN-VIP}.

At this stage, \kl{CN} implementers could experiment with different inference
schemes with a strong safety net. The mechanisation could be taken one step
further and inference algorithms themselves could be mechanised, with proofs
that they have desirable properties such as always ending in either a
successful proof or a counter-example. Accomplishing this would require some
non-trivial engineering to connect the proof-assistant to the SMT solver, used
heavily by the inference algorithms.

At the point that even inference is mechanised, the remaining bases of trust
would be (a) the SMT solver and (b) the accuracy of the traces. The former could be
checked  by asking for certificates of proofs from the solver.\sidenote{Thus
avoid the need to reimplement an SMT solver inside a proof assistant, even
if it is only for decidable theories.} The latter would require mechanising
\kl{Core} and its dynamic semantics.

Of course, mechanising anything is hard, and so extracting verified code from
it. Still, if possible, the latter is what I judge most valuable; a parallel
implementation and proof would also work but they would require a manual,
trusted synchronising over time. I also expect mechanisation to be increasingly
valuable as we add support for concurrency, higher-order resources, fractional
permissions. % chktex 17 (from the soundness statements)
